module v2.workflow.floor2_prepared_subject

import v2.std.algebra { fold_list, length, list_snoc_item }
import v2.std.collection { List }
import v2.std.logic { Bool }
import v2.std.node { Symbol }
import v2.std.text { String }

// FLOOR2 — the floor as ONE prepared subject and N claims against it, modeled
// here rather than in the runner. The seed retains exactly two host primitives:
// build an evaluable context from a source set, and evaluate one qualified name
// in it. Which witnesses run, what identity each has, what refuses, and what the
// run's verdict is are facts of this module.
//
// WHY THE MODEL EXISTS AT ALL, in one measured sentence: the runner spent 1927.6s
// preparing 1137 entry-private compiler worlds to enable 148.1s of evaluation
// (run 31745991795, resolve_ms grouped once per entry against summed
// eval_wall_ms). One preparation serving every claim is the same work with the
// entry removed from the key.

type PreparedSubjectSpec {
  source_roots: List<String>
  exclusions: List<String>
}

// The exclusion set is PART OF THE SUBJECT, so it is declared beside the roots and
// never borrowed from a roster that answers a different question. The probe exclusion
// roster is pattern rows UNION a derived strict-resolve failure closure, and its
// pattern half exists to keep witnesses OUT of a probe over production code. Borrowed
// here it prepared 1611 of 3621 modules and 9057 of 9317 witnesses could not find
// their own code — a subject that resolves cleanly while being the wrong subject.
//
// THERE IS NO GROUND MEANING "IT FAILED TO RESOLVE". Two arms were deleted rather than
// kept for completeness: ExcludedDerivedFailureClosure readmitted the self-growing
// closure under a name, and a generic ExcludedCannotStrictResolve let an accidental
// breakage wear the same badge as a deliberately invalid fixture — which is exactly
// the move that hid ~150 undeclared imports for however long. The remaining two grounds
// say what the case IS, not what happened when it was tried:
//
// A deliberately-invalid fixture carries the WITNESS that consumes its invalidity. If
// nothing reads the refusal, the file is not a fixture, it is a broken module, and the
// field is what makes that distinction checkable instead of asserted. An out-of-roots
// module carries the root that WOULD contain it, so the exclusion names its own
// dissolution: add that root and the row is unnecessary.
type SubjectExclusionGround
  = ExcludedDeliberatelyInvalidFixture { property_witness: Symbol }
  | ExcludedOutsideDeclaredSourceRoots { containing_root: Symbol }

// THE SITE CARRIES ITS MODULE, because the host already knows it.
//
// `entry` and `module_path` are read from ONE inventory entry in one iteration -- the host
// parsed the file to prepare the subject, so the file's module is in hand at the moment the site
// is built. Carrying only `entry` here forced the manifest to ship a second 3,680-row
// entry-to-module list into the interpreter and JOIN it back, re-deriving inside the fold a fact
// the producer held two lines earlier.
//
// It also made a refusal for a state that cannot exist. Both lists were projected from the same
// inventory keyed by the same file path, so every site's entry appeared exactly once and the
// join's answer was always "exactly one" -- `ModulePathNotUnique` had no reachable producer, and
// two complete floor runs emitted zero identity refusals. Carrying the module here is the
// construction that makes the absent case unrepresentable rather than validated (DESIGN 4b:
// construction over validation), which is why the refusal arms below are gone rather than kept
// "just in case".
type WitnessSite {
  entry: String
  function: String
  module_path: String
  reads_live_tree: Bool
}

// WITNESS IDENTITY IS `module.function`, NEVER the bare function name.
//
// The host resolves an invocation through a name-keyed map. Per-entry that is
// safe: one context holds one closure, so one name means one node. Under a
// SHARED prepared subject it is not — two entries declaring the same test fn name
// collapse to a single map entry, so one runs twice and the other never runs, AT
// AN UNCHANGED ROW COUNT. The receipt is then indistinguishable from a healthy
// run, which is silent wrongness rather than a degradation.
//
// Measured on the live roster: 30 names covering 63 rows. Predicted first from
// the CI receipt at the same 30/63 and reproduced by execution.
//
// Qualification is CONSTRUCTION, not validation: the ambiguous call has no
// representation here, so no consumer can express it. The refusal arms below are
// only for the residue where identity genuinely cannot be derived.
type WitnessIdentity {
  module_path: String
  function: String
}
fn witness_identity_qualified_name(identity: WitnessIdentity) -> String {
  concat(concat(identity.module_path, "."), identity.function)
}

// WHICH EXECUTION KEY A BATCH GETS IS FLOOR POLICY, so it is a row here rather than a
// condition in the seed. The note above claims changing floor policy is a row edit and not
// a Rust edit; landing the routing rule only in cli_run would have made that sentence false
// in the same change that wrote it.
//
// ONE INPUT DECIDES, and it used to be two. An earlier revision of this rule also read
// roster provenance -- walked from scan_dirs versus named by the batch -- because a batch
// naming ten entries would have paid a whole-tree preparation to serve them, which is the
// entry-major cost-shape defect pointing the other way. That ground was real and it is now
// dissolved rather than merely unused: ExecutionMode is not an input to resolution, so the
// seed memoizes the resolved graph by its source digest and a second consumer over the same
// tree pays a file walk and builds its own context. The distinction bought nothing once the
// cost it named was gone, so FloorRosterProvenance and the
// WholeTreePreparationNotAmortized ground are DELETED here rather than left standing beside
// the climb that obsoleted them.
type FloorSelectionPosture
  = NoSelectionMachinery
  | SelectionRequiresPerEntryFrontier

// The surviving entry-major arm names its own dissolution in its constructor: a
// selection-bearing lane evaluates a per-entry frontier, and an entry-private context is
// what supplies one. It retires when node_frontier_selection leaves Runnable, at which point
// this coproduct collapses to a single inhabitant and deletes with it.
type FloorExecutionKey
  = OnePreparedSubjectKey { subject: PreparedSubjectSpec }
  | EntryMajorUntilFrontierRetires

fn floor_execution_key(
  posture: FloorSelectionPosture,
  subject: PreparedSubjectSpec
) -> FloorExecutionKey {
  match posture {
    SelectionRequiresPerEntryFrontier => EntryMajorUntilFrontierRetires
    NoSelectionMachinery => OnePreparedSubjectKey { subject: subject }
  }
}

fn floor_execution_key_is_one_prepared_subject(key: FloorExecutionKey) -> Bool {
  match key {
    OnePreparedSubjectKey { subject: _ } => true
    EntryMajorUntilFrontierRetires => false
  }
}

// A DECLARED RUNG REGRESSION, measured on this change rather than discovered after it.
//
// Under one prepared subject every module of the tree is present at once, which is the most
// permissive name pool that can exist. An authored probe -- a provider, a control module that
// declares the import, and a module reaching the same symbol with none -- showed the
// undeclared reference BINDS. So the required floor no longer refuses a cross-module
// reference whose module is not imported.
//
// WHAT THE PREVIOUS RUNG ACTUALLY WAS, stated carefully because the obvious reading inflates
// it. The entry-major path was not strict either: DESIGN's Class B records that a stripped
// file's bare references resolved exactly when some unrelated import elsewhere in the
// assembled closure had already dragged the target into the pool -- binding by pool
// coincidence, described there as accidental coverage rather than a per-file property. So
// this is not strict becoming permissive. It is a NARROW accidental pool becoming a WIDE
// deliberate one, and one thing genuinely improves: binding no longer depends on which
// unrelated entries happen to share a closure, so the same module binds the same way on
// every run.
//
// WHAT GETS WORSE, and it is the reason this row exists: the ambient-pool class is now
// undetectable by the floor, where before it was detectable by accident. A module that
// declares no import for a symbol it uses will never red here.
//
// POPULATION: cross-module references not covered by a declared import. Bounded and already
// under repair -- gunbc#8268 declares direct imports for the UriScheme consumers.
//
// RESTORATION TRIGGER, and it is NOT that repair. Emptying the population does not raise the
// rung, because the floor would still accept a newly written undeclared reference. The rung
// is restored when resolution enforces declared-import narrowing independently of pool
// breadth -- the namespace-only lane's terminal step, where import becomes a parse error and
// dependencies derive from container.member references. Until then this class sits at
// mitigatable and the exact-entry check is the only thing that sees it.
type FloorBindingStrictnessRegression {
  previous: Symbol
  current: Symbol
  population: Symbol
  restoration_trigger: Symbol
}

data floor2_binding_strictness_regression: FloorBindingStrictnessRegression = FloorBindingStrictnessRegression {
    previous: ^narrow_accidental_pool_binding,
    current: ^whole_subject_pool_binding,
    population: ^cross_module_references_without_a_declared_import,
    restoration_trigger: ^resolution_enforces_declared_import_narrowing
  }
